BCC_Q({"y": 2016, "n": 8, "part": "B", "topic": "opt", "title": "Collecting Wood", "answer": 2, "story": "<p>During his descent from the mountain top, the beaver, Theseas, is\ncollecting wood for his lodge from several stations. Every station holds\na different amount of wood. While he is descending, he cannot change\ndirection and start climbing again, that is, he can only go in the\ndirections of the arrows.<\/p>\n<p>The paths between stations are given in the image below. Every circle\nis a station and the number in the circle represents the amount of wood\navailable at that station.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows. \" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:60%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdescb')\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Collecting Wood<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdescb\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>The circles and the beaver are placed into six rows and arranged to form an equilateral triangle shape. The beaver is in row 1 (top row), there are two circles in row 2, three circles in row 3, four in row 4, five in row 5, and six in row 6. An arrow goes from the beaver to each of the two circles in row 2.  An arrow goes from each circle to the two circles closest to it in the row below. Each circle is labelled with a number from 1 to 5. The numbers in the circles, in order, in each row are as follows: <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Row 2: 3, 2 <\/li>\n<li>Row 3: 4, 1, 5 <\/li>\n<li>Row 4: 2, 5, 5, 1 <\/li>\n<li>Row 5: 1, 3, 1, 5, 5 <\/li>\n<li>Row 6: 5, 4, 5, 3, 4, 5<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>What is the maximum total amount of wood that Theseas can collect\nduring his descent?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>19<\/li>\n<li>20<\/li>\n<li>21<\/li>\n<li>22<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(C) 21<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>The path is shown in the image below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The following circles are highlighted: second circle in row 2 labelled 2, third circle in row 3 labelled 5, third circle in row 4 labelled 5, and fourth circle in row 5 labelled 5, the fifth circle in row 6 labeled 4. They are connected by arrows and form a path from the beaver to the bottom row.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:60%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>To see why there is no better path, notice that this path has a score\nof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(21\\)<\/span>. Any better path cannot\ncontain a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> since the path length\nis <span class=\"math inline\">\\(5\\)<\/span> and if all the other stations\nhad <span class=\"math inline\">\\(5\\)<\/span> logs, the total number of\nlogs would be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(5+5+5+5+1 = 21\\)<\/span> which\nis not bigger than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(21\\)<\/span>. Removing the\nones from the diagram and looking at all remaining possible paths gives\nthe paths: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\\{3,4,2,3,4\\}, \\{3,4,2,3,5\\},\n\\{3,4,5,3,4\\}, \\{3,4,5,3,5\\}, \\{ 2,5,5,5,3\\}, \\{2,5,5,5,4\\}\\]<\/span> and\nof these the paths, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{2,5,5,5,4\\}\\)<\/span>\ngives the unique best score of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(21\\)<\/span>.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>In a triangular pyramid with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span>\nlevels (the given problem is an example of a 6 level pyramid), there\nwill be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{n-1}\\)<\/span> different paths\nfrom the top to the bottom. For large values of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, this gives too many possibilities to\ncompute by hand or even by machine! Instead of calculating the total sum\nof every path, we can add to every station (or <em>node<\/em>) above the\nbottom level the maximum value of its two children, the nodes connected\nat a lower level. For example, let’s say we have the following pyramid\nwith <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)<\/span> levels:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"There is one circle, labelled 2, in level 1 (top level). There are two circles, labelled 3 and 5, in level 2. An arrow connects the circle in level 1 to each circle in level 2. There are three circles, labelled 6, 1, and 2, in level 3. An arrow points the circle labelled 3 in level 2 to the circles labelled 6 and 1 in level 3. An arrow points the circle labelled 2 in level 2 to the circles labelled 1 and 2 in level 3.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Then we can reduce the pyramid by using the above algorithm to get a\nsmaller pyramid given by the following graph, where 9 comes from adding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)<\/span> to\nthe maximum of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(6\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> and similarly <span class=\"math inline\">\\(7\\)<\/span> comes from adding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(5\\)<\/span> to the maximum of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)<\/span>. Repeating this procedure reduces this\npyramid to one node with value 11. Breaking down a problem into smaller\nsubproblems is known as <em>dynamic programming<\/em>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"There is one circle, labelled 2, in level 1 (top level). There are two circles, labelled 9 and 7, in level 2. An arrow connects the circle in level 1 to each circle in level 2.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:30%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>", "mc": 1});